A||||18||Songman||Black box for NASCAR||||1||
Z||000000||Songman||08-22-2001||09:13 AM||||Well.. what do you guys that like NASCAR think about the crash recorder box that NASCAR is going to install? And what do you guys that don't like NASCAR think about it?<P>To me, there is too much hoopla being made over a crash. It happens. But somebody always has to place the blame. I know it is all done in the name of making the cars safer for the drivers and that is a good thing. I just hope that is the real reason for the things they do and not some underlying motives. I know the sponsors and suppliers and everyone have reputations and businesses to protect at all cost.<P>Now they are saying the the seat belts weren't installed in the car properly. So the blame comes down to the pit crew. No ones business is hurt. Personally, and this is just an opinion, I don't buy it. Neither do I think the belts were faulty. Nor do I think anyone was at fault. It was just a freak accident. And I'm also not sure that Dale didn't black out like he did once before. I'm not sure he wasn't dead before he hit the wall and that's what made him wreck! Just my opinions though.. I'd love to hear what was being said on the radio at the time of the crash...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||84Bill||08-22-2001||09:44 AM||||Why not? they already have them in Indy cars and have helped in the design of safety features such as the energy absorber on the back of the trans. Prior to this many Indy drivers were suffering mysterious injuries when hitting a wall backwards. <BR>I personally dont want a black box in my car. The way I see it NASCAR drivers test new technology and get paid well to do so. Let them be our "Crash test dummys"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||08-22-2001||10:40 AM||||I'm with 84Bill.  Black boxes are great for race cars, but bad for street cars...too easy for Big Brother to start giving tickets based on black-box readings, or worse yet, implement 65 mph governors on our cars.<P>People are impatient with NASCAR being so close-mouthed about this whole thing, but they have to understand that anytime experiences a tragic and untimely death, there are hundreds of salivating ambulance-chasing lawyers eager to make some fast bucks off the deal.  I think Mike Helton and co. have been silent in hopes of keeping the lawyers at bay -- not to "save face" or stir controversy.<P>I understand NASCAR is quietly looking into ways to build "crush zones" into the front of the car, without compromising safety in any other type of crash.  But they'd better do it quietly or Kenny Irwin's third cousin twice removed will show up with some oily scumbag lawyer, hoping to get rich.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||08-22-2001||10:47 AM||||No way, No how, are you going to make racing 100% safe. This Black box thing is an example of NASCAR reacting to the media frenzy that occurred after the crash.<BR>The black box will give very little information that's not retrievable from the video. Earnhart received two different impacts. The wall and then the other car. My guess is the second impact caught him and the harness out of position to absorb the the force. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||08-22-2001||01:53 PM||||The whole thing is silly. The thing that makes sports both interesting to watch and exciting to participate in is the risk involved.<P>That's not to say that unneccessary risks should be eliminated or mitigated wherever possible. But it's gonna get to the point where race cars are gonna look like ugly '70's cars with huge front and rear bumpers and NASCAR will just be bumper cars with 400 horses under the hood.<P>Why not just remove the drivers from the vehicles altogether ? Remotely piloted vehicles ?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||08-22-2001||02:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by deceler8:<BR><B>The whole thing is silly. The thing that makes sports both interesting to watch and exciting to participate in is the risk involved.<P>That's not to say that unneccessary risks should be eliminated or mitigated wherever possible. But it's gonna get to the point where race cars are gonna look like ugly '70's cars with huge front and rear bumpers and NASCAR will just be bumper cars with 400 horses under the hood.<P>Why not just remove the drivers from the vehicles altogether ? Remotely piloted vehicles ?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The thing about taking the drivers out of the cars came up earlier in another post.. I don't think the drivers would want us taking their careers away from them. Plus, I don't think the fans would go for it. That is why R/C racing is good locally but it not a national sport..<P>And I was thinking earlier that if they keep on with all of this safety stuff it will be like tanks out there... no one wants to see that.. NASCAR is very safe. There have been a relatively small number of drivers killed in 50+ years. The risk is part of the sport - for both the drivers and the spectators.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Butter||08-22-2001||04:19 PM||||Black Box,<BR>Oh yea that thing what tells you what went wrong when a plane crashes.  Well that might be ok if it can be done discreatly and only to the car.  But I have heard some news that they would also like to connect the driver's physical body also.  I do not think that should be done.  Truth be known I'd bet some of the past winners would be pulled from the race before they had the chance to win it.  My cousin what races once told me about a race he barely won when he and one of the folks that helped him set up his car and sold him his motor went around the track side by side and never touched for about 15 laps.  After they come into the pits his friend come over to congratulate him on some awesome driving and he had a nose bleed from all the excitement.  I believe the winners of most real car racing are pushing ALL the limits.  I can see the head line now on Earnhart.  "Earnhart pulled from race for dying on the track, black box indicated he was under stress and made a mistake" or maybe something like "Black box indicated Earnhart couldn't win so he bangs head on wall".  Sounds like more Big Brother to me. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||08-22-2001||05:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Butter:<BR><B>But I have heard some news that they would also like to connect the driver's physical body also.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ford started that back in the 80's. Why do you think they called it the "Probe"?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Galen||08-22-2001||06:57 PM||||Factory flaws happen  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Heck, just look at Ford.. They made the probe..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/sig.gif">  Ray -    <A HREF="mailto:galen@ezrpg.com">galen@ezrpg.com</A>    - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>www.FieroLinks.f2s.com</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - ICQ: 98668558||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroMontreal||08-22-2001||07:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Factory flaws happen   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Heck, just look at Ford.. They made the probe..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let's not take cheap shots at the probe... Some people actually like em (not me though).. Remember the probe msg board.. we made peace with em ..so we should keep it friendly..||||reg||1
Z||000010||frontal lobe||08-22-2001||08:25 PM||||Are they going to put a #3 on the black box?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Pontiaddict||08-22-2001||09:51 PM||||Why don't they start making race cars out of black box material?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>Why not? they already have them in Indy cars and have helped in the design of safety features such as the energy absorber on the back of the trans. Prior to this many Indy drivers were suffering mysterious injuries when hitting a wall backwards. <BR>I personally dont want a black box in my car. The way I see it NASCAR drivers test new technology and get paid well to do so. Let them be our "Crash test dummys"</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I remember seeing that TLC show on indy car safety. They said that the car companies were looking at indy cars to study crashes instead of stockcars because indy car crashes were simpler and usually involved sliding into the wall. Where a winston cup crash usually involves more than one car and lots of other variables that they have to take into account to get any usefull information from it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-23-2001||08:27 PM||||First off... IRL & CART use of "Black Box" units has affected changes to more than the car. The data has also been used to alter the tracks as well.<P>Black Boxes report in much finer detail and greater accuracy than the video method NASCAR had to resort to. Had the boxes been present in stock cars at Daytona they would have known in hours to days the information that took weeks to guess at from the video. They would have know exact speeds and angle of the various inpacts.<P>I've studied all three reports currently available on the belt. I'm still working that info over. I think the belt fail was a combination of factors. I think it's easier to have a misadjusted belt than Simpson, NASCAR, and other testing facilities have realized or admitted. I would not be suprised that if prior to this a misadjusted belt was more comon than a correct belt. Now NASCAR, CART, and IRL will be paying closer attention to these things so hopefully that problem will be reduced.<P>Would I use a Simpson belt on a track? Absolutely. I'd pay allot more attention to the installation instructions than I would have before and I'd be allot more carefull about checking that those adjusters aren't getting cocked on the belt. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroKyle||08-24-2001||12:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMontreal:<BR><B> Let's not take cheap shots at the probe... Some people actually like em (not me though).. Remember the probe msg board.. we made peace with em ..so we should keep it friendly..</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh by my understanding we are still at war with the Probe guys...... HaHa Galen!! I think Ford was a mistake all together. Lets see what cars they have ruined, the Mustang, Thunderbird, and thats about it but they are big ones. But they were in the top 10 best U.S. sports cars until they blew it with bigger uglier T-Birds and Mustangs.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||ChadMan||08-24-2001||09:50 AM||||You are right on about Simpson belts, Ogre.  Bill Simpson has retired from the safety-equipment business over this...and considering all the lives his equipment has saved, that's almost as big a tragedy as Earnhardt's death.||||reg||9
